Data Types: Used to specify type of the data.
----------
- Data types are divided into 2 main categories --> Primitive, Reference/Non-Primitive
Under Primitive --> Integral, Decimal, Character, Boolean
Under Non-primitive --> Arrays, Strings,Classes
Primitive Data Types:
--------------------
1. Integral Data Type -> These are used to store integer type number without decimal points
                         We can store both positive and negative numbers using integral data types.
						 We have 4 data types in this category -> byte, short, int  and long
						 -byte --> Default value = 0 --> 1 byte
						 -short --> Default value = 0 --> 2 byte
						 -int --> Default value = 0 --> 4 bytes
						 -long --> Default value = 0 --> 8 bytes
						 eg: long balance = 30000000;
2. Decimal Data Types -> Used to store numbers with decimal values (Floating values)
                         We can use both positive and negative values
						 In this category, we have 2 data types
						 -float --> 4 bytes --> upto 6 decimal points
						 -double --> 8 bytes --> upto 15 decimal points
						 Note: Whenever we store any decimal value, compiler will think by default as a double value. 
						       8 bytes required to store it and we storing into 4 bytes
							   float a = 25.01;  //error
							   float a =25.01f;
3. Character Data Types -> used to store single character
                           Any single character(Alphabet/digits/special characters) We  can store using 'char' data type
						   Char data type will occupy 2 bytes of memory (In C language, 1 byte as ASCII values and Unicode values also)
						   When we are storing data into 'char' data type variable single quote is mandatory. Default value is '\u0000'
						   eg: char gender = 'm';
						       char rank = '1'   --> correct
							   char rank = '10'  --> Not correct, only one character from 0 to 9.
4. Boolean Data Types --> Used to store only True or False value. Occupies only 1 bit value.
                          A true is represented as 1 , and false as 0 (In binary language)
						  Default value for boolean is False.
						  eg: isPass = true;
Note: 8 bits = 1 byte

TYPES OF JAVA COMMENTS
======================
1) Single-line Comment
----------------------
-> Used for short explanations.
-> Compiler ignores everything after // on that line.
Syntax:
    // comment
Example:
    // This is a single-line comment
    int x = 10;

2) Multi-line Comment
---------------------
-> Used for writing comments in multiple lines.
-> Starts with /* and ends with */
Syntax:
    /*
       comment
    */
Example:
    /*
      This is a multi-line comment
      used for longer descriptions.
    */
    int y = 20;


3) Documentation Comment (Javadoc)
----------------------------------
-> Used to generate official Java documentation.
-> Mostly written before classes, methods, and constructors.
-> Starts with /** and ends with */
Syntax:
    /**
     * comment
     */
Example:
    /**
     * Adds two numbers
     * @param a first number
     * @param b second number
     * @return sum
     */
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

-Identifiers: Java components -> packages, class, method, variables
-------------
-All Java components requires a name, which is called Identifiers
-Java Provided few rules to work with identifiers
